<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/inventions?focus=olive-oil">Inventions &amp; Discoveries</a> · Farming and food</p><h1 class="enc-title">Olive oil</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Olive oil is the oil pressed from olives, a staple of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> diet, lighting and trade since antiquity. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/macedonian-empire">Macedonian Empire</a> it was among the products of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>'s agrarian economy. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire</a> imported it from the Mediterranean world in exchange for African goods.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/olive-oil.jpg" alt="Olive oil" width="320" height="299" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Poyraz 72 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Oliven_V1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Olive oil is a vegetable oil obtained by pressing whole olives, the fruit of Olea europaea, a traditional tree crop of the Mediterranean Basin. Olive trees have been cultivated around the Mediterranean since the 8th millennium BCE, and wild olives, which originated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asia-minor">Asia Minor</a>, were collected by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> people as early as that time. The exact date and place of domestication are unclear. The modern olive tree may have originated in ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a> and spread to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> and later to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-africa">North Africa</a>, though some scholars argue for an Egyptian origin.</p><p>Archaeological evidence in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galilee">Galilee</a> shows that olives were being turned into oil by 6000 BCE, and at a now-submerged settlement south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a> by 4500 BCE. The archives of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/city-state">city-state</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ebla">Ebla</a> also mention oil production.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">How it was used</h2><p>Besides food, olive oil served for religious rituals, medicines, fuel in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oil-lamp">oil lamps</a>, soap-making and skincare. The Spartans and other <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> rubbed themselves with oil while exercising in the gymnasia. Its cosmetic use began early in the 7th century BCE, spread to all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greek-city-states">Greek city-states</a> together with athletes training naked, and lasted close to a thousand years despite its great expense.</p><p>The olive tree has long symbolised peace between nations. In Greek mythology the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> was named after the goddess <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athena">Athena</a> because her gift of an olive tree was held to be more precious than the salt spring offered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poseidon">Poseidon</a>. In Jewish observance, pure oil from the first pressing was the only fuel permitted for the seven-branched menorah, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabernacle">Mishkan</a> and later in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/temple-in-jerusalem">Temple in Jerusalem</a>. Various Christian churches use it in consecration ceremonies.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Olives were traditionally ground into paste by large millstones, and the paste was then pressed. In the ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a> three methods produced different grades. The finest oil came from ripe olives harvested from the top of the tree and lightly pressed, since what flows from light pressure is very sweet and thin, while inferior oil came from olives left to soften or shrivel. Pomace, the remaining solid waste, has been used as fuel since Roman times, as it burns hotter and with less smoke than <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charcoal">charcoal</a>. Olive oil is one of the three core food plants of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> cuisine, with wheat and grapes.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Olive oil” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>From the second century <b>olive oil</b> became a major export, carried in African amphorae, and the fine red tableware now called African Red Slip ware was traded across the Mediterranean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/africa-proconsularis">Africa Proconsularis · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In return, Aksum imported textiles, glassware, wine, <b>olive oil</b> and metalwork from the Mediterranean world, and spices, cotton and gems from India.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aksumite-empire">Aksumite Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Most citizens farmed, and <b>olive oil</b> was the chief agricultural export, while Attic black-figure and then red-figure pottery dominated Mediterranean markets in the sixth and fifth centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/athens">Athens · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Salted fish from the strait and the Sea of Azov, hides, slaves and honey were also exported, and in return came wine, <b>olive oil</b>, fine pottery and metalwork.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bosporan-kingdom">Bosporan Kingdom · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>The coastal plains and the Sahel produced grain and <b>olive oil</b>, and the tribes raised sheep and camels; wool, hides and leather were major exports.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Wheat, wine, <b>olive oil</b> and especially wool from transhumant flocks were the main products, and Zaragoza became the commercial and political centre.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-aragon">Kingdom of Aragon · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="inv-entry"><h2 class="enc-section-title">On the inventions tree</h2><dl class="inv-entry-facts"><div><dt>First known</dt><dd>by 6000 BCE</dd></div><div><dt>Where</dt><dd>Galilee</dd></div></dl><p class="inv-entry-note"><span class="inv-k">Note</span>The exact date and location of olive tree domestication are unclear; wild olives are reported to have been collected by Neolithic people as early as the 8th millennium BCE, and that the earliest surviving olive oil amphorae date to 3500 BCE in Early Minoan times.</p><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/inventions?focus=olive-oil">See it on the inventions tree &rarr;</a></p></section>
